<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  M2 (MOBILE_DUAL_MODE_PLAN): layout facts the CODE needs, expressed as resources so the
  qualifier decides them — no mode plumbing through fragments.

  ⭐ M11: every flag here is a DEVICE question, so the defaults below are the PHONE answers and
  `values-television` carries the TV ones. They used to be split `values` (TV) / `values-port`
  (phone), and that was wrong for the same reason `ui_uses_dpad_focus` was: **`-port` asks "is this
  screen portrait?", not "is this a phone?"**. Rotate a handset and every one of these silently
  flipped to the TV answer — the Live screen lost its channel list, the bottom bar became a side
  rail, the source sheet stopped closing on a backdrop tap. `-television` matches a TV in either
  orientation and never matches a phone, which is the question actually being asked.
-->
<resources>
    <!-- Top-level navigation: a bottom bar on a phone, a vertical rail on TV. -->
    <bool name="home_nav_is_horizontal">true</bool>
    <!-- M3: browse category list — a top chip rail on the phone, a left rail on TV. -->
    <bool name="browse_categories_are_horizontal">true</bool>
    <!--
      M5: the play circle on a source row. On TV it is a FOCUS affordance — it appears on
      the focused row and marks where CENTER will act. A touch device has no focused row,
      so the same rule would hide it forever; there it is a permanent tap affordance.
    -->
    <bool name="source_row_play_always_visible">true</bool>
    <!--
      M5: tapping the dimmed area behind a sheet closes it. TV leaves this off on purpose —
      there is nothing to tap, and attaching a click listener would make the backdrop
      clickable, which lets it into the D-pad focus order and adds a dead stop to the
      ladder. BACK already closes the panel there.
    -->
    <bool name="dismiss_on_backdrop_tap">true</bool>
    <!--
      M7: which Live screen a "live" hop opens WHEN THE USER HAS NOT CHOSEN. The EPG guide is a
      multi-day timeline grid and needs width; the classic screen is a channel list with a
      preview above it. TV keeps the guide, a phone gets the list. An explicit choice in
      Settings still wins on both.
    -->
    <bool name="live_defaults_to_classic">true</bool>
    <!--
      M7b: may a list row take focus FROM A TOUCH? On TV, yes — focus is the whole interaction
      model. On a phone it is the wrong model outright (the rulebook in CLAUDE.md says one tap
      acts), and a row that grabs focus on ACTION_DOWN can have its click cancelled when the
      list scrolls the newly-focused row into view. Rows stay `focusable` in both cases; this
      only governs focus acquired by touching.
    -->
    <bool name="rows_focusable_in_touch_mode">false</bool>
    <!-- M6: Settings categories — a top chip rail on the phone (there is no room for two
         columns at 411dp), a vertical rail beside the detail panel on TV. -->
    <bool name="settings_categories_are_horizontal">true</bool>
    <!--
      M8: the selected channel's guide. On a phone it is the plan's "day timeline per channel" —
      the same data (viewModel.guideEpg is already the whole day) read DOWN the page. On TV it is
      a horizontal strip of tiles beside the preview. The TV's own EPG grid is not portable:
      EpgGridView handles onKeyDown and nothing else, so on a touchscreen it would render and
      then ignore every finger.
    -->
    <bool name="epg_strip_is_horizontal">false</bool>
    <!--
      M7c: may screen code MOVE FOCUS on its own? On TV that is the interaction model — focus is
      remembered, restored after a refresh, and routed by D-pad. On a touchscreen it is the
      opposite of the rulebook: a tap must act, and a poster that takes focus instead of opening
      is exactly why every card needed two taps.
    -->
    <bool name="ui_uses_dpad_focus">false</bool>
</resources>
